# The harness reads these from the BOUND REPO's .env (e.g. ~/Sources/beadgame/.env)
# or from real environment variables. Real env vars win.

# --- required for voice ---
# ElevenLabs key. The permission it needs is **Text to Speech**, and only that.
#
# ⚠ This changed. The old path spoke inside a Speech Engine session, so the key
# needed the row labelled "ElevenAgents" and explicitly did NOT need TTS. The
# harness now calls text-to-speech directly, so a key that worked for years
# answers 502 until you add that permission.
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=sk_...

# Whose voice she speaks in.
HARNESS_VOICE_ID=...

# --- optional ---
# A Speech Engine is no longer used to speak or to listen. It survives as ONE
# thing: somewhere to read a voice id from, so an existing setup keeps sounding
# like the same person. Setting HARNESS_VOICE_ID above makes it unnecessary — and
# then the key needs no ElevenAgents permission either.
# SPEECH_ENGINE_ID=seng_...
#
# Which TTS model. NOT the engine's: eleven_v3_conversational is rejected by the
# standalone endpoint, and a realtime model is what you want when time-to-first-
# sound is the experience (160ms warm). ⚠ Flash predates v3 audio tags, so tags
# are stripped unless this names a v3 model.
# HARNESS_TTS_MODEL=eleven_flash_v2_5
#
# HARNESS_REPO=/path/to/the/repo/this/instance/binds/to
# HARNESS_PORT=4620
# HARNESS_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# HARNESS_DIRECTOR_PLAN=plans/2026-07-30-director-consolidation.md
# How tool permissions resolve. "auto" (the default) lets a model classifier settle
# the ordinary ones and escalate only what it will not take on itself; "default"
# asks for everything the settings files do not pre-approve; "acceptEdits" lets
# file edits through; "dontAsk" never asks and denies anything not pre-approved.
# This matters more here than in a terminal: an approval card CANNOT be answered by
# voice, so every prompt stops a spoken conversation until you are back at the page.
# ("bypassPermissions" is deliberately unavailable — see src/config.ts.)
# HARNESS_PERMISSION_MODE=auto
# What the UI calls the director ("Beth wants to use Bash"). Read from the bound
# repo's .claude/DIRECTOR.md — set this only if that file names her some other way.
# HARNESS_DIRECTOR_NAME=Beth
# How much of what she writes is read ALOUD. The transcript always shows all of
# it — this only decides what is pronounced, because six paragraphs of real code
# work is seconds of skimming on the page and a minute and a half of unskippable
# audio. "brief" (default) speaks her `say` items in full plus the LAST PARAGRAPH
# of a longer reply; "full" speaks everything; "headlines" speaks only findings,
# events and short in-progress lines; "off" speaks nothing at all — genuinely
# nothing, with no line held back for later. Switchable live from the strip, and
# it applies whether or not the mic is on: she speaks without being spoken to.
# HARNESS_SPEECH_LEVEL=brief
# Reasoning effort while the MIC IS OPEN, restored when it closes. Spoken
# conversation trades depth for latency; typed work keeps full effort. "off"
# disables the switch.
# HARNESS_VOICE_EFFORT=low
# How long the words must stop CHANGING before a spoken turn is sent. Served to
# the page, which is where the window runs. A recogniser revises as you speak, and
# acting on each revision turns one sentence into several turns. Raise it if turns
# fire mid-sentence; lower it if replies feel sluggish. (Note the rule is about the
# words changing, not about events arriving — see ui/listen.js.)
# HARNESS_VOICE_SETTLE_MS=2500
# Whether she remembers the PERSON between sessions — what you mentioned in
# passing, so she can follow it up later. "off" means genuinely off: the tools are
# not registered, nothing is recorded, and nothing rides the prompt. Someone who
# disables this is saying don't keep a file on me, not "ask me less often".
# The file lives in ~/.director-harness/<repo>/personal.jsonl, never in the repo.
# HARNESS_PERSONAL=off

# --- the test monitor ---
# What to run to answer "is the tree green". Leave unset and it is DETECTED from
# what the project already declares (package.json scripts.test, *.csproj,
# Cargo.toml, go.mod, pyproject.toml, a Makefile test: target). The harness never
# invents a command, because a guessed command is a shell execution nobody
# authorised — a repo it does not recognise simply has no runner until you name
# one here. ⚠ Split on whitespace and spawned directly; nothing reaches a shell.
# HARNESS_TEST_CMD=bin/ci --suite unit
#
# ⚠ Running is OFF until you enable it once per repo, in the page, with the
# detected command shown first. It executes project code on a schedule, and a
# suite that touches the network, spins a container or costs money must not start
# because you happened to save a file.
#
# It runs only when the tree has CHANGED, has SETTLED, and she is IDLE. How still
# the tree must be first — a suite run against a half-finished edit is a red light
# that means nothing:
# HARNESS_TEST_SETTLE_MS=5000
# Floor between runs, so a rapid series of saves does not queue one per keystroke:
# HARNESS_TEST_MIN_INTERVAL_MS=120000
# After this a run is killed and REPORTED as timed out, rather than left hanging:
# HARNESS_TEST_TIMEOUT_MS=300000

# Every launch is a fresh conversation. Set 1 to resume the previous one instead.
# HARNESS_RESUME=1
#
# --- where this file goes ---
# Three layers, most specific first: real environment vars, then the BOUND REPO's
# .env, then ~/.director-harness/.env.
#
# The ElevenLabs credentials belong in the MACHINE file. There is one account for
# this Mac, so copying them into every repo duplicates the same secret — and
# forgetting to means the new repo silently runs text-only, whose only symptom is a
# caution icon after you speak. A project wanting its own voice can still override
# any key in its own .env.
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.director-harness
#   grep -E '^(ELEVENLABS_API_KEY|HARNESS_VOICE_ID)=' \
#     <a-repo>/.env > ~/.director-harness/.env && chmod 600 ~/.director-harness/.env
#
# HARNESS_DIRECTOR_PLAN is the exception — it is per-repo, so keep it in the repo.

# --- exposure ---
# EVERYTHING listens on loopback: the UI, the API, and her audio. Nothing dials in
# and there is no second port, so this harness is unreachable from off the machine
# by construction — which is what makes a shell-executing handoff safe to have.
# Set this only if you want the UI on your LAN, and understand that it publishes
# /api/turn to anyone who can reach it.
# HARNESS_BIND=0.0.0.0
